OUTREACH-ATTEMPT-CAP-1 -- terminalize a twice-failed email lookup
=================================================================
Target file : qnfo-cloud-ops/worker.js   (main = "worker.js" per wrangler.toml)
Defect      : D3 -- needs-email self-loop / LIMIT-10 starvation
Filed by    : qnfo-ops, 2026-09-30
Related     : #1294 (resolved, OUTREACH-QUEUE-SELECTOR-DRIFT-1), #1508 (closed, falsified)

WHY THIS IS NOT LANDED AS A DIRECT COMMIT
-----------------------------------------
worker.js is a single 126,602-byte deployed bundle with no src/ directory. The
GitHub write tools available to qnfo-ops accept FULL file content only (no
partial/offset edit), and no read tool returns the whole file in one window
(repo read caps at 20k chars, workspace read at 100k). Re-emitting 126KB by hand
into a production bundle risks silent corruption of a worker that runs scheduled
fleet jobs. Applying this patch from a container is also impossible: the
container has no push credentials. This patch file is therefore committed to a
branch for review and one-command application.

THE DEFECT
----------
Selector (worker.js:1547):
  SELECT id, paper_id, author, email, reason FROM outreach_queue
   WHERE status IN ('pending','needs-email') ORDER BY created_at ASC LIMIT 10

Failure branch (worker.js:1562-1566):
      if (!email) {
        out.skipped_no_email++;
        await env.AUDIT.prepare("UPDATE outreach_queue SET status='needs-email', error='email lookup failed (HTML+e-print)' WHERE id=?1 AND status='pending'").bind(r.id).run().catch(function() {
        });
        continue;
      }

Trace for a row whose lookup fails:
  run 1: row is 'pending' -> UPDATE fires (guard status='pending' holds) -> row becomes 'needs-email'.
  run 2: row is re-SELECTED (selector includes 'needs-email'), verifyArxivEmail is
         called again (a real arXiv HTML + e-print fetch), and if it fails again the
         guard status='pending' NO LONGER holds, so NO status write occurs -> `continue`.
  run N: identical to run 2. The row never leaves the work set.

Two consequences:
  1. Wasteful/amplifying network load: verifyArxivEmail is re-run against arXiv on
     every drain, forever, for each stuck row. This is the mechanism that can
     trigger arXiv rate-limiting, which then degrades lookups for healthy rows.
  2. Starvation: ORDER BY created_at ASC means these permanently-old rows sit at the
     FRONT of the LIMIT 10 window and are re-selected every run, displacing the
     pending rows behind them.

Current blast radius: LATENT. At the 2026-09-30 probe there were 0 rows in
'needs-email' state (outreach_queue = 22 pending / 27 skipped / 20 sent), so no row
is currently affected. The defect arms itself the next time a radar-hub candidate
with email IS NULL fails resolution.

THE PATCH (exact, one hunk)
---------------------------
Apply with:
  python3 - <<'PY'
  import io
  p = "qnfo-cloud-ops/worker.js"
  s = io.open(p, encoding="utf-8").read()

  OLD = """          out.skipped_no_email++;
          await env.AUDIT.prepare("UPDATE outreach_queue SET status='needs-email', error='email lookup failed (HTML+e-print)' WHERE id=?1 AND status='pending'").bind(r.id).run().catch(function() {
          });
          continue;"""

  NEW = """          out.skipped_no_email++;
          /* OUTREACH-ATTEMPT-CAP-1: a row that fails lookup while ALREADY in
             needs-email has now failed twice; terminalize it. Without this the row
             stays inside the selector ('pending','needs-email') forever, re-running
             verifyArxivEmail on every drain and occupying one of the LIMIT 10 slots
             (ORDER BY created_at ASC), which starves newer pending rows. */
          await env.AUDIT.prepare("UPDATE outreach_queue SET status='skipped-no-email', error='email lookup failed twice (HTML+e-print)' WHERE id=?1 AND status='needs-email'").bind(r.id).run().catch(function() {
          });
          await env.AUDIT.prepare("UPDATE outreach_queue SET status='needs-email', error='email lookup failed (HTML+e-print)' WHERE id=?1 AND status='pending'").bind(r.id).run().catch(function() {
          });
          continue;"""

  assert s.count(OLD) == 1, "expected exactly 1 occurrence, found %d" % s.count(OLD)
  io.open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8").write(s.replace(OLD, NEW))
  print("patched OK")
  PY
  node --check qnfo-cloud-ops/worker.js   # bundle must still parse

Why two statements rather than one: the two guards are mutually exclusive
(status='needs-email' vs status='pending'), so exactly one fires per row and the
row is guaranteed to leave the work set on its second failure. A row gets exactly
one retry, preserving recovery from a transient arXiv failure.

NO SCHEMA CHANGE: this deliberately avoids adding an `attempts` column to
outreach_queue. The existing status value itself encodes "attempt 1 done", so the
fix is two statements and zero migrations.

VERIFICATION PLAN (must be run after deploy)
--------------------------------------------
  SELECT status, COUNT(*) n FROM outreach_queue GROUP BY status;
  -- expect no unbounded growth of 'needs-email'; any row that reaches
  -- 'skipped-no-email' is terminal and must not be re-selected.
  SELECT id, status, error FROM outreach_queue WHERE status='needs-email';
  -- expect this set to be strictly transient (empty after the following drain).

ADVERSARIAL / KNOWN LIMITATIONS
-------------------------------
1. Two strikes may be too few if arXiv is degraded for an extended window: a row
   could be terminalized while the outage is ongoing, losing a recoverable contact.
   Mitigation is manual re-queue (paper_id and reason are retained on the row).
   A schema-backed attempt counter with backoff would be strictly better; it is
   out of scope here precisely because it needs a migration.
2. The patch does not address why radar-hub inserts candidates with email IS NULL
   in the first place. Fixing the producer (resolve or terminalize at insert time)
   would remove this class entirely and is the better long-term fix.
3. `node --check` validates syntax only. It does NOT prove the statement guards are
   semantically correct against live D1. The guards must be confirmed by the
   verification queries above.
4. This file was authored from a read of main @ b801d33. If worker.js has changed
   since, the assert will fail closed rather than patch the wrong region -- re-derive
   the OLD block before applying.
